WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.752
Residual connections answer an important engineering question:

00:00:03.752 --> 00:00:09.687
after attention changes the token representation, do we throw away the original input?

00:00:09.687 --> 00:00:14.482
Usually, no. We add the attention output back to the input.

00:00:14.482 --> 00:00:20.057
In a simple expression, the next representation is x plus attention of x.

00:00:20.057 --> 00:00:24.787
A normalization step is then applied according to the architecture’s design.

00:00:24.787 --> 00:00:32.512
This creates a direct path through the network so information does not have to survive only through every

00:00:32.512 --> 00:00:32.952
transformation.

00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:40.602
Residual paths also make very deep networks easier to optimize because gradients have a more direct route

00:00:40.602 --> 00:00:40.902
backward.

00:00:40.902 --> 00:00:48.592
In code, the shapes must match before addition, which is another reason the attention output returns to the

00:00:48.592 --> 00:00:48.987
same model dimension.

00:00:48.987 --> 00:00:55.492
Modern Transformer families differ in whether normalization occurs before or after certain sublayers,

00:00:55.492 --> 00:01:02.272
often called pre-norm and post-norm designs, but the core purpose remains stable information flow through

00:01:02.272 --> 00:01:03.610
repeated blocks.
